WEBVTT

00:00.702 --> 00:02.883
- [Voiceover] So what
we have here is a circle

00:02.883 --> 00:05.400
with the center at Point O,

00:05.400 --> 00:07.457
and then we have a tangent
line to the circle.

00:07.457 --> 00:09.431
Let me actually label this line.

00:09.431 --> 00:11.776
Let's call this Line L.

00:11.776 --> 00:15.066
And we see at Point A is the point

00:15.066 --> 00:18.469
that the tangent line
intersects with the circle,

00:18.469 --> 00:20.120
and then we've drawn a radius

00:20.120 --> 00:23.130
from the center of the circle to Point A.

00:23.130 --> 00:25.268
Now what we want to do in this video

00:25.268 --> 00:28.865
is prove to ourselves that this radius

00:28.865 --> 00:33.370
and that this tangent line
intersect at a right angle.

00:33.370 --> 00:34.879
We want to prove to ourselves

00:34.879 --> 00:37.774
that they intersect at a right angle.

00:37.774 --> 00:40.174
And the first step to doing that

00:40.174 --> 00:41.477
is we're going to feel good,

00:41.477 --> 00:44.497
we're going to prove to
ourselves that Point A

00:44.497 --> 00:48.438
is the closest point on Line L

00:48.438 --> 00:51.168
to the center of our circle.

00:51.168 --> 00:53.333
So I want to prove,

00:53.333 --> 00:57.677
prove that Point A

00:57.677 --> 01:01.845
is closest, closest point

01:02.628 --> 01:06.268
on L to Point O.

01:06.268 --> 01:08.010
To Point O.

01:08.010 --> 01:09.580
And I encourage you to pause the video

01:09.580 --> 01:12.893
and see if you can prove that to yourself.

01:12.893 --> 01:14.607
Well, to think about
that, just think about

01:14.607 --> 01:16.592
any other point on Line L.

01:16.592 --> 01:19.109
Pick any other arbitrary point on Line L.

01:19.109 --> 01:20.615
It could be this point right over here.

01:20.615 --> 01:22.172
It could be this point right over here.

01:22.172 --> 01:23.458
It could be this point right over here.

01:23.458 --> 01:27.162
And you immediately see that
it sits outside of the circle.

01:27.162 --> 01:28.792
And if it's sitting outside of the circle,

01:28.792 --> 01:30.071
I'll pick this point here,

01:30.071 --> 01:32.810
just so it'll become a little
bit clearer on our diagram,

01:32.810 --> 01:35.227
if it's sitting outside of the circle,

01:35.227 --> 01:37.918
in order to get from
Point O to this point,

01:37.918 --> 01:41.244
I'm going to call this
Point B right over here,

01:41.244 --> 01:44.792
you have to go the length of the radius,

01:44.792 --> 01:46.939
you have to go the length of the radius,

01:46.939 --> 01:49.123
and then you have to go some more.

01:49.123 --> 01:52.049
So this length, the length of Segment O B,

01:52.049 --> 01:55.077
is clearly going to be longer
than the length of the radius,

01:55.077 --> 01:56.511
because you have to go to the radius

01:56.511 --> 01:58.630
to get to the circle itself

01:58.630 --> 02:00.348
and then you have to
go a little bit further

02:00.348 --> 02:02.645
for any point that sits
outside of the circle.

02:02.645 --> 02:04.652
So Point A is the only point,

02:04.652 --> 02:06.292
by definition this is a tangent line,

02:06.292 --> 02:08.660
it's the only point
that sits on the circle.

02:08.660 --> 02:12.860
Every other point on Line L
sits outside of the circle,

02:12.860 --> 02:14.581
so it's going to be further.

02:14.581 --> 02:17.507
So Point A, hopefully
this makes you feel good,

02:17.507 --> 02:18.645
because you pick any other point,

02:18.645 --> 02:19.805
it's going to sit outside of the circle,

02:19.805 --> 02:21.663
so you have to go to the
radius and then some.

02:21.663 --> 02:24.420
So hopefully this makes
you feel good that Point A

02:24.420 --> 02:29.247
is the closest point on L
to the center of the circle.

02:29.897 --> 02:33.039
Now, we're not done yet, we
now have to convince ourselves

02:33.039 --> 02:35.884
that if we have a point and a line,

02:35.884 --> 02:37.862
that the segment connecting the point

02:37.862 --> 02:42.440
to the closest point on the
line, to that original point,

02:42.440 --> 02:45.362
that, that's going to be
perpendicular to the line.

02:45.362 --> 02:47.919
So let me give ourselves some space here.

02:47.919 --> 02:51.862
We want to prove, we want to prove

02:51.862 --> 02:55.095
that if we, that the segment,

02:55.741 --> 02:59.022
segment connecting,

02:59.022 --> 03:03.940
connecting a point off the line,

03:04.470 --> 03:06.305
off the line,

03:06.305 --> 03:08.916
and closest point on the line,

03:08.916 --> 03:12.580
closest point on the line,

03:13.381 --> 03:15.485
is perpendicular,

03:15.485 --> 03:19.772
is perpendicular to the line.

03:21.102 --> 03:23.239
So what we want to do,
is we want to say, hey,

03:23.239 --> 03:25.204
if we have some line here,

03:25.204 --> 03:27.326
if we have some line here, L,

03:27.326 --> 03:30.888
and if you were to take
a point off the line,

03:30.888 --> 03:33.829
so let's say, so you wanted
a point off the line,

03:33.829 --> 03:36.246
so let's say that, that's
this point right over here,

03:36.246 --> 03:39.423
Point O, and you want the
segment connecting the point

03:39.423 --> 03:42.984
off the line to the
closest point on the line.

03:42.984 --> 03:45.861
So the closest point on the line,

03:45.861 --> 03:49.407
so let's say that this is the
closest point on the line,

03:49.407 --> 03:52.594
we want to feel good that
this segment connecting them,

03:52.594 --> 03:54.710
so let me do this in a new color,

03:54.710 --> 03:56.211
that the segment connecting them

03:56.211 --> 03:58.758
is going to be perpendicular to the line,

03:58.758 --> 04:00.947
that it goes just straight down like that,

04:00.947 --> 04:03.761
that it's going to be perpendicular.

04:03.761 --> 04:06.184
And I'm gonna prove this by contradiction.

04:06.184 --> 04:07.984
I'm going to assume that
it's not perpendicular.

04:07.984 --> 04:10.573
So assume,

04:10.573 --> 04:13.811
assume that the segment,

04:14.531 --> 04:16.322
segment connecting,

04:17.363 --> 04:19.151
connecting, this is kind of wordy,

04:19.151 --> 04:23.536
a point off line

04:24.183 --> 04:27.283
and closest point to the line,

04:27.283 --> 04:30.159
closest point to line,

04:31.899 --> 04:35.877
is not, is not perpendicular

04:36.510 --> 04:39.480
to the line.

04:39.480 --> 04:41.536
So how can I visualize that?

04:41.536 --> 04:44.592
Well, I could draw my
line right over here.

04:44.592 --> 04:47.136
So that's Line L,

04:47.136 --> 04:51.118
and let's say I have my
Point O right over here.

04:51.118 --> 04:54.630
Point O, and let's say the
closest point on Line L

04:54.630 --> 04:57.570
to Point O, let's say that it's not,

04:57.570 --> 04:58.902
so let's say it's over here,

04:58.902 --> 05:00.783
that if I were to
connect these two points,

05:00.783 --> 05:03.035
that it's not perpendicular to Line L.

05:03.035 --> 05:04.405
So this is the closest point,

05:04.405 --> 05:06.364
let's call this Point A,

05:06.364 --> 05:08.514
and let's say that the
segment connecting these two

05:08.514 --> 05:11.580
is not perpendicular to the line.

05:11.580 --> 05:16.580
So let me get, so let's assume
this is not perpendicular.

05:16.618 --> 05:19.358
So this angle, this angle,

05:19.358 --> 05:24.327
this angle right here, is
not, is not 90 degrees.

05:25.217 --> 05:26.672
So if we assume that,

05:26.672 --> 05:28.940
the reason that this is going
to be a proof by contradiction

05:28.940 --> 05:31.474
is I can show that if
this is not 90 degrees,

05:31.474 --> 05:34.477
that I can always find a point
that is going to be closer,

05:34.477 --> 05:37.288
another point on Line L
that is going to be closer

05:37.288 --> 05:40.120
to Point O, which contradicts the fact

05:40.120 --> 05:42.158
that this was supposed
to be the closest point,

05:42.158 --> 05:44.937
A was supposed to be the
closest point on Line L to O.

05:44.937 --> 05:47.646
And how do I always find a closer point?

05:47.646 --> 05:49.823
Well, I construct a right triangle.

05:49.823 --> 05:52.519
I can construct a right triangle,

05:53.456 --> 05:55.323
just like this.

05:56.456 --> 05:59.991
I can construct a right
triangle like that,

05:59.991 --> 06:01.826
and we see that this distance,

06:01.826 --> 06:04.194
let's call this distance
right over here a,

06:04.194 --> 06:07.143
and we could call the
base of this triangle b,

06:07.143 --> 06:08.814
let me do this in a different color.

06:08.814 --> 06:13.738
So a, b, that's the base
of the right triangle,

06:13.738 --> 06:17.127
and the hypotenuse is
the distance from O to A.

06:17.127 --> 06:19.038
We could call that c.

06:19.038 --> 06:21.655
We know from the Pythagorean Theorem

06:21.655 --> 06:24.511
that a squared plus b squared,

06:25.111 --> 06:26.807
plus b squared,

06:26.807 --> 06:30.013
let me do this, plus b squared,

06:30.013 --> 06:32.440
is going to be equal to c squared.

06:32.440 --> 06:35.566
Is going to be equal to c squared.

06:35.566 --> 06:37.150
And so b squared,

06:37.150 --> 06:39.347
if we have a non-degenerate
triangle right over here,

06:39.347 --> 06:43.065
this is going to be some
positive value over here,

06:43.065 --> 06:45.939
and so a is going to be less than c.

06:45.939 --> 06:48.827
So this gets us to the conclusion,

06:48.827 --> 06:51.491
because if this is some
positive value here,

06:51.491 --> 06:53.070
and a and c are positive,

06:53.070 --> 06:54.626
everything are positive distances,

06:54.626 --> 06:57.245
then this tells us that
a has to be less than c.

06:57.245 --> 06:59.522
That a non-hypotenuse
side of a right triangle,

06:59.522 --> 07:01.080
of a non-degenerate right triangle,

07:01.080 --> 07:02.539
assuming it has some area,

07:02.539 --> 07:04.413
is going to be shorter
than the hypotenuse.

07:04.413 --> 07:06.573
The hypotenuse is the longest side.

07:06.573 --> 07:10.497
So a is going to be less than c,

07:10.497 --> 07:12.816
which would tell us, if a is less than c,

07:12.816 --> 07:14.693
that we've found another point,

07:14.693 --> 07:16.242
let's call this point, I don't know,

07:16.242 --> 07:17.574
I've used a lot of letters here,

07:17.574 --> 07:18.889
let's call that Point D.

07:18.889 --> 07:21.072
D is going to be closer.

07:21.072 --> 07:22.821
D is closer.

07:23.421 --> 07:27.560
D is closer.

07:27.796 --> 07:30.137
So we've just set up a contradiction.

07:30.137 --> 07:34.406
We assumed A was the closest
point on Line L to Point O,

07:34.406 --> 07:36.568
but we assumed the segment connecting them

07:36.568 --> 07:38.277
is not at a 90-degree angle.

07:38.277 --> 07:40.100
If it's not at a 90-degree angle,

07:40.100 --> 07:43.358
then we can drop a perpendicular
and find a closer point,

07:43.358 --> 07:45.266
which is a contradiction to the fact

07:45.266 --> 07:47.927
that A was supposed to
be the closer point.

07:47.927 --> 07:50.565
So this leads to a contradiction.

07:51.155 --> 07:53.457
Contradiction.

07:53.457 --> 07:56.911
Contradiction.

07:56.911 --> 07:58.927
Because you can actually
find that this is not

07:58.927 --> 08:01.860
the closest point, you can
always find a closer point,

08:01.860 --> 08:04.724
so therefore, the segment
connecting a point off the line

08:04.724 --> 08:08.872
to the closest point to the
line must be perpendicular.

08:08.872 --> 08:11.216
Must be perpendicular.

08:11.216 --> 08:15.039
So the segment connecting
a point off the line,

08:15.039 --> 08:17.676
to a closest point on the line,

08:17.676 --> 08:21.239
that must be perpendicular to the line.

08:21.239 --> 08:23.599
Must be perpendicular to the line.

08:23.599 --> 08:26.985
And just like that, we hopefully
feel good about the idea

08:26.985 --> 08:28.681
that if you have a radius,

08:28.681 --> 08:30.566
and the point at which it intersects

08:30.566 --> 08:32.890
a tangent line to the circle,

08:32.890 --> 08:35.884
that, that forms a 90-degree angle.

08:35.884 --> 08:38.999
The radius and the tangent line.
